  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "cites_only",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper evaluates downstream benchmark performance at multiple model sizes, which bears on V6, but does not test the power-law loss or compute-allocation claims.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports held-out cross-entropy for parameter-matched architectures at 50M, 152M and 369M, but does not fit power laws, extrapolate from smaller runs, or vary token counts and compute to find an optimum. Those CE results therefore do not test V1–V3. Its architecture comparisons do not isolate depth or width across a wide range at fixed parameter count (V4); it tests neither other modalities or training regimes (V5) nor fit dependence (V7). Downstream results are recorded for V6.",
    "test_reason": "This is an architecture paper proposing SISA attention. It trains models at three sizes (50M, 152M, 369M) on a fixed 10B tokens to compare architectures. It fits no power law, makes no prediction from smaller runs, does no compute-allocation search and has no controlled shape sweep. It never tests whether loss or downstream scores scale predictably, and does not cite either original. Scale is also confounded with recipe: depth, width and learning-rate schedule all change across sizes, and the 369M Mamba-3 changes architecture. The size-wise rise in LAMBADA is incidental, with three points and no fit. It does not test V6's 'predictably'. No enum value means 'neither tests nor uses', so cites_only is the nearest; the paper does not actually cite the originals.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Mostly correct, but incomplete. The authors conclude that SISA has the lowest held-out CE among attention-only models at 50M, 152M and 369M, though its margin over FoX narrows to 0.004 at 369M. They find SISA is the only attention model that gains from context beyond 2K, and that with their kernels it trains faster and leaner than baselines. Samba and Mamba-3 (and Hymba at equal tokens) reach lower CE at larger scale. Zero-shot benchmarks are treated as supplementary, and whether SISA's lead holds at billion scale is open.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The paper proposes and evaluates a new attention architecture; it does not fit or extrapolate the original scaling-law curves or optimize parameter-token allocations.",
    "method_sensitivity": "",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "For every architecture run at all three sizes, held-out CE falls and LAMBADA rises from 50M to 152M to 369M on 10B tokens (Table 1). SISA's LAMBADA goes 16.9→21.4→23.1. The paper says HellaSwag, ARC-Easy and PIQA rise above chance with scale, while five other benchmarks stay near chance.",
        "proposed_claim": "Within a fixed-token, small-scale (50M–369M) architecture comparison, loss and some downstream scores improve monotonically with model size. No functional form is fitted.",
